-        <p>For \(k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots\), let \(a_k = 2^{-(k+1)}\) and \(Q_k = (a_k, 2a_k] \times [0, a_k]\). Then \(2a_{k+1} = a_k\), so the \(x\)-interval of \(Q_{k+1}\) ends where the \(x\)-interval of \(Q_k\) begins, and the blocks are pairwise disjoint. Each \(Q_k\) lies in \([0,1]^2\), since \(2a_0 = 1\). On \(Q_k\) we have \(x > a_k \ge y\), so \(f \ge 0\) on \(Q_k\) by Assumption 2. Let \(c_k = \int_{Q_k} f\). By Assumption 3 on the closed rectangle \([a_k, 2a_k] \times [0, a_k]\), the inner integral is \(\int_0^{a_k} f(x,y)\,dy = G(a_k) - G(0) = a_k/(x^2 + a_k^2)\), with \(G\) as in the proof of Proposition 3.1. Then \(c_k = \int_{a_k}^{2a_k} a_k/(x^2 + a_k^2)\,dx\). Substitute \(x = a_k t\): \(c_k = \int_1^2 dt/(1 + t^2) = \arctan 2 - \arctan 1\). This does not depend on \(k\), so \(c_k = c = \arctan 2 - \pi/4\). Since \(f^{+} \ge f\,\indic_{Q_0 \cup \dots \cup Q_{N-1}}\) on the square, Assumption 4 gives \(\int_{[0,1]^2} f^{+} \ge N c\) for every \(N\). Since \(c > 0\), the integral is infinite. Swapping \(x\) and \(y\) turns \(f\) into \(-f\) and maps the square to itself, so the same argument with the blocks reflected gives \(\int f^{-} = \infty\).</p>
