             <span class="problem-title">R2 · Symmetric truncation</span>
             <p>Let \(S_\epsilon = [0,1]^2\) minus the open disk of radius \(\epsilon\) about the origin. Show that \(\int_{S_\epsilon} f = 0\) for every \(\epsilon \in (0,1)\), and explain why this does not make \(f\) integrable on the square.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>On \(S_\epsilon\), \(f\) is bounded and continuous, so its integral exists. The swap \(\tau(x,y) = (y,x)\) maps \(S_\epsilon\) onto itself, and \(f(\tau(x,y)) = -f(x,y)\). The integral is invariant under this measure-preserving map, a standard fact used without proof (listed in §9), which gives \(\int_{S_\epsilon} f = \int_{S_\epsilon} f\circ\tau = -\int_{S_\epsilon} f\), so \(\int_{S_\epsilon} f = 0\). The value \(0\) is the limit of the truncated integrals, but Proposition 3.2 shows that \(\int |f| = \infty\) on the full square. A limit of integrals over truncations does not make \(f\) integrable, because the integrability in Assumption F is of \(|f|\) on the whole space.</p>
