-      <p class="why-it-matters">A filtration is the record of what is known at each time, and a martingale is a process whose best forecast of its next value, given that record, is its current value. It is the formal version of a fair game. This lesson defines both on a finite tree. It proves that the fair \(\pm 1\) walk is a martingale on its natural filtration, and that the biased walk is not, because the next step averages \(2p - 1\) given the record, where \(p\) is the up-step probability (§2, Proposition 2.3). Lab 1 checks both exactly on all 1024 paths of a ten-step tree. The stopping times of L09 and the bounded optional stopping theorem of L10 build on these.</p>
+      <p class="why-it-matters">A filtration is the record of what is known at each time, and a martingale is a process whose best forecast of its next value, given that record, is its current value. It is the formal version of a fair game. This lesson defines both on a finite tree. It proves that the fair \(\pm 1\) walk (defined in §2) is a martingale on its natural filtration (Definition 1.2), and that the biased walk is not, because the next step averages \(2p - 1\) given the record, where \(p\) is the up-step probability (§2, Proposition 2.3). Lab 1 checks both exactly on all 1024 paths of a ten-step tree. L09 (stopping times) and L10 (optional stopping) build on these.</p>
 
       <h2 id="s1"><span class="section-number">1</span>Information over time</h2>
 
-      <p>Let \(\Omega\) be a finite set of paths, with weights \(p(\omega) > 0\) that sum to 1. A <em>process</em> is a finite sequence \((X_0, X_1, \dots, X_N)\) of random variables on \(\Omega\). For a fixed \(k\), the record of the first \(k\) values \((X_0, \dots, X_k)\) partitions \(\Omega\) into cells, one for each possible record. A cell of this partition is an event that is known once the record is known. The partition is the information at time \(k\).</p>
+      <p>Let \(\Omega\) be a finite set of paths, with weights \(w(\omega) > 0\) that sum to 1. A <em>process</em> is a finite sequence \((X_0, X_1, \dots, X_N)\) of random variables on \(\Omega\). For a fixed \(k\), the record of the first \(k\) values \((X_0, \dots, X_k)\) partitions \(\Omega\) into cells, one for each possible record. A cell of this partition is an event that is known once the record is known. The partition is the information at time \(k\).</p>
 
